\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces MGLPDSNet. Top left: \textcolor {red}{[6]} untied V-cycles, each receiving the preprocessed measurement $\tilde  {\bm  {y}}=\bm  {E}^H(\bm  {y}-\mu \bm  {E}\bm  {1})$, $\bm  {E}$ and $\sigma $; the first layer starts from $\bm  {x}=\tilde  {\bm  {y}}$, $\bm  {z}^{(0)}=\Pi _{\bm  {\lambda }}(\bm  {A}^H\tilde  {\bm  {y}})$. Top right: one V-cycle over three grid levels; each level solves its own problem $(\tilde  {\bm  {y}}_l,\bm  {E}_l,\sigma _l)$, tilted by the FAS correction $\bm  {\pi }^{(l)}$. Middle: an LPDS Layer, with $\tilde  {\bm  {x}}=\bm  {x}^{+}+\theta (\bm  {x}^{+}-\bm  {x})$; $\eta ,\theta ,\bm  {\lambda }$ are affine in $\sigma $; dashed inputs (the FAS terms) exist on coarse levels only. The Coarse Correction acts on $\bm  {u}\in \{\bm  {x},\bm  {z}\}$ with $\hat  {\bm  {u}}_c$ the coarse level's output and $\bm  {u}_c$ its restricted input. Bottom: the FAS-Correction, in the order its operations run; it carries its own learned copies of $\bm  {A}^H$, $\bm  {B}$ and $\bm  {\lambda }$.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
